kernel	Run cargo deny check	2026-10-22T10:51:18.0526446Z       
kernel	Run cargo deny check	2026-10-22T10:51:18.0526838Z       On the multi-threaded runtime, there is a race condition between `Interval::poll_tick` and the runtime's time driver.
kernel	Run cargo deny check	2026-10-22T10:51:18.0526921Z       
kernel	Run cargo deny check	2026-10-22T10:51:18.0527137Z       This affects `Interval` only. `Sleep` and `Timeout` are **not** affected.
kernel	Run cargo deny check	2026-10-22T10:51:18.0527222Z       
kernel	Run cargo deny check	2026-10-22T10:51:18.0527304Z       ## Details
kernel	Run cargo deny check	2026-10-22T10:51:18.0527387Z       
kernel	Run cargo deny check	2026-10-22T10:51:18.0528753Z       In Tokio v1.54.0, a race condition between `tokio::time::Interval::poll_tick` (including `.tick().await`) and the runtime time driver can drop an internal timer entry without synchronizing with the driver thread that is firing it. This causes a data race and use-after-free on the timer's waker and task state in safe Rust, leading to assertion panics, task reference-count corruption, or heap memory corruption.
kernel	Run cargo deny check	2026-10-22T10:51:18.0529015Z       
kernel	Run cargo deny check	2026-10-22T10:51:18.0529453Z       This bug was introduced by https://github.com/tokio-rs/tokio/pull/8458, and it was fixed by https://github.com/tokio-rs/tokio/pull/8692.
kernel	Run cargo deny check	2026-10-22T10:51:18.0529537Z       
kernel	Run cargo deny check	2026-10-22T10:51:18.0529618Z       ## Impact
kernel	Run cargo deny check	2026-10-22T10:51:18.0529697Z       
kernel	Run cargo deny check	2026-10-22T10:51:18.0530032Z       This may lead to crashes and use-after-frees in applications using `Interval` on a multi-threaded runtime.
kernel	Run cargo deny check	2026-10-22T10:51:18.0530110Z       
kernel	Run cargo deny check	2026-10-22T10:51:18.0530444Z       Since this issue is known to lead to crashes in production systems, the v1.54.0 release of Tokio has been yanked.
